  "proofs": [
    {
      "proof_id": "HO_PERIODIC_WEIGHTED",
      "title": "Periodic Hessian access and weighted on-demand access",
      "statement": "With weight $w\\ge1$ and period $b\\asymp w$, simulations preserve information and relate total cost to periodic call count up to explicit constant and initialization terms.",
      "body": "The periodic interface returns a gradient on every call and a Hessian additionally on calls b,2b,...; the first Hessian is not prepaid. The on-demand interface charges $W=c_gN_g+c_gwN_H$.\n\nTo simulate an on-demand algorithm using the periodic interface, answer a gradient request at the next call. For a Hessian request, repeat the desired point until the next Hessian slot and discard unwanted responses. Each Hessian request costs at most b calls, so $T\\le N_g+bN_H$. With $b=\\lceil w\\rceil$, $T\\le2W/c_g$. A periodic lower bound therefore yields a weighted lower bound.\n\nConversely, simulate a periodic algorithm by purchasing a gradient each step and a Hessian at its scheduled slots. Then $N_g+wN_H\\le T+w(T/b+1)$. With b comparable to w this is $O(T+b)$, and the initialization term must remain explicit. Both simulations preserve adaptivity and output requirements; no operation is free merely because it is discarded or used while waiting. In the primary definition, use the semantics of the action types when their numeric labels conflict with nearby notation.",
      "scope": "Only the stated periodic and on-demand interfaces, with total weighted cost including gradients. Does not identify separate resource-coordinate bounds with one jointly attainable budget.",
      "limitations": "The mapped bound retains its complete model, oracle, algorithm view, guarantee, quantity, resource projection, and parameter-region conditions. This argument does not enlarge that scope.",
      "algorithm_scope": "Only the stated periodic and on-demand interfaces, with total weighted cost including gradients. Does not identify separate resource-coordinate bounds with one jointly attainable budget.",
      "source_refs": [
        "SO_SRC_e2895bd8b68c2f5f"
      ]
    }
